# EYES — being in the room, and the one row the whole design ordered itself around.
#
# ⚠ *"Looking straight up, sky pixels: 0 (today: all of them)"* is the design's own
# measurement row, and it was written when the roof had no underside. That is why
# EYES is last on the list: until a ceiling existed, a first-person view indoors was
# a picture of the sky through it. The row is the soffit's payoff, and the OUTDOOR
# station is the control that says the instrument can still find sky when there is
# some — without it, "0" would also be what a broken camera reports.

echo --- flat ground
at 0 0 0
verb raise
wait rebuilt

echo --- a house where the author is looking
at 0 -4 90
verb place
wait rebuilt

send 27:1
send 40:4

echo --- THE CONTROL: outdoors, looking STRAIGHT UP. This must be sky and nothing else
at 0 -10 90
send 3:0,-20000
step 120
# ⚠ EYES IS FIRST PERSON, so the eye sits ON the character — 0.959 — and FOLLOW does
# not. The two bands are disjoint, so a mode that silently fell back to FOLLOW breaks
# one of them.
cam 0.7 1.3
snap s15-eyes-up-outside
# ⚠ `sky 1` IS NOT A ROUNDING. Straight up outdoors is sky and NOTHING else, which is
# why this is the control: if the pitch fence stopped the look short, something else
# is in shot and every row below is measuring the wrong frame.
frame 0 1.0 sky 0.95 1.0

echo --- indoors, looking STRAIGHT UP: a ceiling, and no sky at all
at 0 0 90
step 120
cam 0.7 1.3
snap s15-eyes-up-inside
# The same look, indoors: the roof's underside fills it and there is no sky at all.
frame 0 1.0 soffit 0.95 1.0 sky 0 0.001

echo --- indoors, straight DOWN: the floor, and no sky through it either
send 3:0,20000
step 120
snap s15-eyes-down
frame 0 1.0 floor 0.95 1.0 sky 0 0.001

echo --- indoors, LEVEL: the room from inside a head
send 3:0,-250
step 120
cam 0.7 1.3
snap s15-eyes-level
frame 0 0.85 masonry 0.60 0.82 soffit 0.20 0.35

echo --- and back to FOLLOW, which fences the pitch again
send 40:1
at 0 0 90
step 150
# ⚠ THE OTHER SIDE OF THE DISJOINT PAIR: out of a head, the eye stands off again.
cam 1.6 2.4
# ⛔ THIS ROW HAD NO `snap`, so it judged the previous station's picture — "a row about
# an unknown scene" by `frame`'s own definition. It has one now.
snap s15-back-to-follow
# ⚠ THE ONLY BAND HERE THAT WAS GUESSED BEFORE IT WAS MEASURED, because the row had no
# picture to measure FROM until it was given one. The guess held; these are the numbers
# it actually reads — masonry 0.5766, soffit 0.2371 — so the row is now measured like
# every other. A band that passes because it is wide is not a check.
frame 0 0.85 masonry 0.48 0.68 soffit 0.16 0.32
